22:27:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The short answer is that different musical cultures adopted different naming traditions. In English-speaking and German-speaking countries, the absolute pitches came to be named with the first seven letters of the alphabet (A–G), while in many Romance-language countries (France, Italy, Spain, etc.), the same pitches are named using the solfège syllables (do, re, mi…). The two systems both go back to medieval times, but they served slightly different purposes and became standardised in different regions. ### Where do the letter names come from? The letter system traces back to the musical theories of Ancient Greece and Rome. In the 6th century, the philosopher Boethius described a two-octave scale using the letters A to O. Later, as the range of Gregorian chant expanded, medieval scholars reduced the alphabet to A–G and repeated it for each octave (A–G, a–g, aa–gg). The note **A** was anchored to the lowest note of the system they inherited, which is why we don’t start on C. This A–G scheme became the standard in Britain, Germany, and eventually North America for naming the fixed pitches of instruments and written music. ### Where do do–re–mi–fa–sol… come from? In the 11th century, the monk Guido d’Arezzo needed a way to teach singers to sight-read. He used the first syllable of each line of the Latin hymn *Ut queant laxis*: > **Ut** queant laxis > **Re**sonare fibris > **Mi**ra gestorum > **Fa**muli tuorum > **Sol**ve polluti > **La**bii reatum, Sancte Ioannes. Each line begins on a successively higher step of the six-note scale (hexachord). The syllables **Ut, Re, Mi, Fa, Sol, La** caught on as a teaching tool. Later “Si” was added (from **S**ancte **I**oannes) and “Ut” was replaced by the more singable “Do” (probably from *Dominus*, Lord, or simply because open vowels are easier). ### Why the split? In much of southern Europe, these syllables didn’t just remain a teaching drill – they **became the absolute note names themselves**. That is, in France, Italy, Spain and many other places, the pitch we call C is permanently named **do**, D is **ré**, E is **mi**, and so on. This is called *fixed-do* naming. It functions exactly like the letter system, just with different labels. In English-speaking countries, the letters remained the official note names (C, D, E…), but the solfège syllables were kept as a **relative pitch system** for ear training and sight-singing. In that *movable-do* system, **do** always means the tonic of whatever key you’re in, not a fixed pitch. That’s why an English speaker might think of “do” as a sound function rather than a specific note – because for them it *is* relative. So the reason you see both: - **Letters (C–B)** = used as the absolute, fixed note names in the Germanic/English tradition. - **Solfège (do–si)** = used either as **absolute** note names in Romance-language cultures *(fixed-do)*, or as **relative** sound-functions in English-language music education *(movable-do)*. Thus it’s not that one is “by sound” and the other “by letter”; it’s that in one cultural area the solfège syllables grew into the permanent names, while elsewhere they remained a tool for describing relationships between notes.
